WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.160
Two cellular floor plans.

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.700
A prokaryote a bacterium is an open plan: few or no internal membranes, no true

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.700
nucleus, most of the work happening in one shared space.

4
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.980
A eukaryote your cells is a building of walled offices: a membrane-bound nucleus,

5
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.440
mitochondria, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, each with its own main jobs.

6
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.940
The banner says it: compartments enable specialization.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.840
That architectural difference is one big reason your cells can do things bacteria

8
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.840
cannot.

9
00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:37.583
The plasma membrane is the cell's outer wall, and it's a phospholipid bilayer.

10
00:00:38.303 --> 00:00:42.143
Hydrophilic heads face the water on both sides; the hydrophobic core in the middle

11
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:43.863
blocks most molecules from crossing.

12
00:00:44.423 --> 00:00:48.963
That makes the membrane a selective barrier impermeable to most solutes on its own,

13
00:00:49.223 --> 00:00:51.603
but studded with proteins that choose what passes.

14
00:00:52.703 --> 00:00:57.523
Channel proteins open passages for specific ions; receptor proteins receive signals

15
00:00:57.523 --> 00:01:01.563
from outside. The membrane decides what gets in and what stays out.

16
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:06.813
Two ways the cell moves cargo across that barrier, and a way we proved it.

17
00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:12.093
On the left, a clinical correlate: in familial hypercholesterolemia, broken

18
00:01:12.093 --> 00:01:17.273
LDL-receptor endocytosis leaves LDL high in the blood, where it builds up in artery

19
00:01:17.273 --> 00:01:19.953
walls endocytosis brings material in.

20
00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:24.693
On the right, the experiment that mapped the path out: Palade and his colleagues'

21
00:01:24.693 --> 00:01:30.813
pulse-chase. Tag fresh protein with a brief radioactive pulse, chase with unlabeled,

22
00:01:31.053 --> 00:01:36.393
and watch the labeled cohort travel from rough ER to Golgi to secretion in snapshots

23
00:01:36.393 --> 00:01:40.073
over time. That's how we know the secretory pathway is real.

24
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:43.637
Here's the chapter's whole argument in one disease.

25
00:01:44.477 --> 00:01:49.277
The lysosome is the cell's recycling center, full of digestive enzymes sealed safely

26
00:01:49.277 --> 00:01:50.277
behind a membrane.

27
00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:54.277
In Tay-Sachs, one of those enzymes — hexosaminidase A — is missing.

28
00:01:54.977 --> 00:01:59.597
Without it, a fatty molecule called GM2 ganglioside can't be broken down, so it

29
00:01:59.597 --> 00:02:04.017
accumulates, the lysosome swells, and the cell, especially nerve cells, chokes:

30
00:02:04.577 --> 00:02:09.017
muscle weakness, neurological decline, and in the classic infantile form, death at

31
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:13.497
around age four. One enzyme, one compartment, one child's life.

32
00:02:14.157 --> 00:02:17.697
The lysosome stays intact, but nothing else can take over the job.
